#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# toon_shading

#% text
# `toon_shading()` is a directive of 3D graphics that asks for flat, cartoon-like shading of the faces that follow.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The 3D renderer shades faces with its own fixed light; `toon_shading` is accepted in graphics and plot options, so code written for other lighting runs, and is not drawn.

#% item
# The call stays as it is when evaluated alone.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
graphics3d([toon_shading(), sphere()])
#% output
#= image("toon_shading.files/toon_shading-1.svgz")

#% cell
plot3d(x*y, [x, -1, 1], [y, -1, 1], plot_style: [toon_shading()])
#% output
#= image("toon_shading.files/toon_shading-2.svgz")

#% cell
toon_shading()
#% output
#= toon_shading()

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The picture is the same without it:

#% cell
graphics3d([orange, sphere()])
#% output
#= image("toon_shading.files/toon_shading-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `plot3d`, `lighting`, `material_shading`
